1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,100
An AI assistant can be helpful with a payment.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,475
But it should not control the money.

3
00:00:05,475 --> 00:00:07,915
SafeBox separates the two.

4
00:00:07,915 --> 00:00:11,450
The assistant connects through an MCP interface.

5
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:15,160
It can check status and prepare a payment request.

6
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:18,970
It cannot sign, approve, or settle through that interface.

7
00:00:18,970 --> 00:00:26,947
The owner sees the exact recipient, amount, purpose, and expiry on their enrolled device.

8
00:00:26,947 --> 00:00:34,009
In our Windows prototype, approval uses Windows Hello and a local fingerprint gate.

9
00:00:34,009 --> 00:00:39,225
The Root and Vault then verify the authorization against the policy.

10
00:00:39,225 --> 00:00:45,276
A fixed ten-minute safety buffer allows time for protective action.

11
00:00:45,276 --> 00:00:53,654
For this demo, time moves on a simulated clock, and settlement is recorded only in a mock ledger.

12
00:00:53,654 --> 00:00:59,567
When suspicious activity is detected, the Risk Engine raises an incident.

13
00:00:59,567 --> 00:01:07,332
An alert appears in a mock outbox, but its link cannot freeze or approve anything.

14
00:01:07,332 --> 00:01:11,447
The owner signs an Emergency Freeze from the console.

15
00:01:11,447 --> 00:01:14,487
While frozen, new payments are refused.

16
00:01:14,487 --> 00:01:18,837
Recovery requires an authorized hold release.

17
00:01:18,837 --> 00:01:25,387
SafeBox is a research prototype, not a production security guarantee.

18
00:01:25,387 --> 00:01:31,252
Independent signing and operating-system isolation remain unfinished.

19
00:01:31,482 --> 00:01:36,772
The principle is simple: let AI prepare.

20
00:01:37,142 --> 00:01:39,922
Keep authority with the owner.
